= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the standard <Achlioptas process> with two independent uniform candidate <edges> per step and a rule choosing one. The <component-band vertex count> $N_{[k,Dk)}$ counts <vertices>, not the number of <graph components>. Integer time rounding changes the arguments below by $O(1)$ steps.

First, at $t_k$ the eventual <graph component> of order at least $\delta n$ is assembled from <graph components> already present at $t_k$. Contract those initial <graph components>. During $s=t_c-t_k$ steps, at most $s$ new <edges> are added, so the eventual connected contracted <graph> contains at most $s+1$ initial <graph components>. Initial <graph components> of order less than $k$ can therefore contribute at most
$$
(s+1)(k-1)\leq\delta n/2
$$
<vertices>, for sufficiently large $n$ and fixed $k$, since $s=\delta n/(2k)+O(1)$. For $k=1$ their contribution is zero. Consequently $N_{\geq k}(G_{t_k})\geq\delta n/2$.

We now use <forced merging of large components>. If at a time $m$ the <graph components> of order at least $K$ contain at least $an$ <vertices>, then after at most $A(a)n/K+O(1)$ steps there is a <graph component> of order at least $an/3$, except on an event of <probability> exponentially small in $n/K$. One explicit choice is $A(a)=81(\log2+2)/a^4$. The proof is a <union bound> over <set partitions> of the at most $n/K$ initial large <graph components>: if all final <graph components> were small, a <balanced component cut> would split their initial <vertices> into sets of size at least $an/3$, and both candidate <edges> cross that fixed cut with <probability> at least $a^4/81$ in every step. The rule cannot avoid such a forced crossing. The failure bound is $e^{-2n/K}$, and a <union bound> makes the estimate simultaneous over all starting times at most $3n$, for each fixed $K$.

Set $a=\delta/4$ and choose a fixed integer $D\geq2$ with $D\geq4A(a)/\delta$. If $N_{\geq Dk}(G_{t_k})\geq\delta n/4$, this lemma would produce a <graph component> of order at least $\delta n/12$ by time
$$
t_k+\frac{A(a)n}{Dk}+O(1)\leq t_c-\frac{\delta n}{4k}+O(1)<t'_c.
$$
That contradicts the assumed $L_1(G_{t'_c})=o(n)$. Thus $N_{\geq Dk}(G_{t_k})<\delta n/4$, and subtraction gives
$$
\boxed{N_{[k,Dk)}(G_{t_k})\geq\delta n/4\quad\text{with high probability}.}
$$
The same $D$ works for every fixed $k$; it depends only on $\delta$, with the number of offered <edges> fixed at two.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <persistence of unsampled graph components> prevents the mass from each size band from disappearing entirely before $t_c$. More precisely, a band $[k,Dk)$ containing at least $\delta n/4$ <vertices> retains at least $\beta n$ <vertices> in that same band after at most $n/k$ steps, <with high probability>, for a fixed $\beta>0$ depending only on $\delta,D$. To see why, each initial <graph component> in that band has a fixed positive <probability> that none of the offered <edge> endpoints touches it during the interval. Such a <graph component> remains unchanged regardless of the selection rule. The <McDiarmid inequality> concentrates the total mass of these untouched <graph components>. The reusable lemma supplies the full uniform-in-starting-time argument, including the version that samples absent <edges>.

Choose finitely many disjoint bands $[D^j,D^{j+1})$, $j=0,\ldots,J$, with $(J+1)\beta>1$. Part (i) provides at least $\delta n/4$ <vertices> in band $j$ at its time $t_{D^j}$. Each interval from that time to $t_c$ has length at most $n/D^j$, so persistence leaves at least $\beta n$ <vertices> in every one of these disjoint bands at the common time $t_c$. A <union bound> over this fixed finite number of bands makes all the conclusions simultaneous. Their total exceeds $n$, a contradiction. **No fixed two-choice Achlioptas rule satisfies the explosive percolation hypothesis.**

This <continuity of fixed-choice percolation> is the fixed-choice obstruction proved by Riordan and Warnke; their https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.5306[original research paper] also treats a broader class of rules. The argument here does not apply when the number of offered choices grows with $n$.
